This migration tree owns the complete PostgreSQL relational schema:

- research-chat conversations, messages, citations, summaries, and agent runs;
- uploaded-document metadata, ingestion jobs, and chunk references;
- draft documents, immutable versions, changes, and context snapshots.

Configure these environment variables in backend/.env before connecting:

    POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
    POSTGRES_PORT=5432
    POSTGRES_USER=lankalawbot
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD=<development-or-secret-manager-value>
    POSTGRES_DB=lankalawbot
    POSTGRES_POOL_SIZE=10
    POSTGRES_MAX_OVERFLOW=5
    POSTGRES_AUTO_CREATE_SCHEMA=false

Keep ``POSTGRES_AUTO_CREATE_SCHEMA=false`` in production. Application startup
then verifies PostgreSQL connectivity and expects versioned migrations to be
applied. Set it to ``true`` only for an explicit local/test bootstrap.

Apply migrations from the backend directory:

    alembic upgrade head

Migrate an existing metadata SQLite file after upgrading the schema:

    python scripts/migrate_sqlite_metadata_to_postgres.py \
        --source database/metadata.sqlite3

Add ``--delete-source-after-verify`` only after reviewing the reported source
and destination row counts. The importer is transactional and refuses data
conflicts instead of silently overwriting PostgreSQL rows.

Generate future revisions only after importing all model modules into
``alembic/env.py``:

    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe schema change"

Do not use auto-creation for production schema changes; use Alembic revisions.
